R version list (admin task)
===========================

The R version dropdown lists module_versions/<cluster>.json, one file per cluster.
Each option's value is the full "module load" argument (toolchain first, e.g.
"GCC/14.3.0 OpenMPI/5.0.8 R_tamu/4.5.2"), so no toolchains are hard-coded in
the environment. The form only reads the file; it never runs "module spider",
which can be slow when a file system is struggling.

Build or refresh the file on each cluster where the environment is offered, on
a node where "module spider" shows what users of that cluster see, and again
after an R_tamu version is installed or removed there:

  ./generate_module_versions.py R_tamu

It writes ../module_versions/<cluster>.json (cluster from clustername; use
--cluster to override, -o for another directory), newest version first, which
is the default in the form. The file is replaced in one step and left alone
when no versions are found. Without a file for a cluster the dropdown says
"Not configured on this cluster" and the job script is not generated.

Other environments: generate_module_versions.py and ../retrieve_module_versions
are not specific to R. Copy both, run the generator with your module name, and
use a dynamicSelect with "retriever": "retrieve_module_versions" and
"autoSelect": "first"; "module load [VALUE]" in the job script then loads the
module with its toolchain.

Package check (admin task, after the version list)
==================================================

The job script preview warns when the main script uses packages that are not
installed for the selected R version. It only lists directories, so it needs
each version's library directories, recorded once per cluster:

  ./generate_r_libraries.py

It loads every version in ../module_versions/<cluster>.json and writes
../module_versions/<cluster>.libs.json (R_tamu's library, the R_LIBS_SITE
bundles, base R, and the personal R_LIBS_USER location under $SCRATCH). Run it
again whenever generate_module_versions.py is rerun or a bundle changes.
Without the file the package check is skipped; nothing else is affected.
